.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-IO-SCHEDULER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-io-scheduler
.Nd set each disk's I/O scheduler once the disks have settled
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
kldload's policy for the kernel's I/O scheduler is:
.Bl -column "rotating disks" "none" -offset indent
.It solid-state disks Ta Ql none Ta
the device's own queues do the work
.It rotating disks Ta Ql bfq Ta
fair queuing, better latency under mixed load
.El
.Pp
A udev rule,
.Pa 60-kldload-scheduler.rules ,
sets it as each disk appears.
On a machine with several disks that write races the disk still being
claimed; the kernel answers
.Dq Device or resource busy ,
and udev does not try again.
.Nm
applies the same policy after boot, to every disk that is not on it, and
reports each change.
Loop, zram, device-mapper, optical devices and ZFS volumes are left alone.
.Pp
It changes nothing already right, so it is safe to run at any time.
.Pa kldload-io-scheduler.service
runs it at boot.
It needs root to change anything; as a user it says so.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the description at the top of the script and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0, even when a scheduler could not be set: that costs performance, never
a boot.
2 for any argument.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop with two NVMe drives on 2026-09-30:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-io-scheduler
kldload-io-scheduler: 0 changed, 0 already correct, 0 could not be set
kldload-io-scheduler: 2 disk(s) not writable here — run it as root
# (exited 0)
# kldload-io-scheduler
kldload-io-scheduler: 0 changed, 2 already correct, 0 could not be set
# (exited 0)
$ cat /sys/block/nvme0n1/queue/scheduler
[none] mq-deadline kyber bfq
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kldload-io-scheduler: DEV: OLD -> NEW (rotational=N)"
Changed.
.It "kldload-io-scheduler: DEV: 'bfq' not available (offers: LIST) — left on 'OLD'"
This kernel does not offer the scheduler for that device.
.It "kldload-io-scheduler: DEV: could not set 'NEW' (still 'OLD') — LIST"
The kernel refused the write.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 4 ,
.Xr udev 7
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-16 a server's six of seven SCSI disks kept
.Ql mq-deadline ,
with one
.Dq Device or resource busy
per disk per boot in the journal, from the distribution's own scheduler
rule as much as from kldload's; setting the same value by hand after boot
worked at once.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: run as a user it touched nothing and
said
.Dq 0 changed, 0 already correct, 0 could not be set ,
as if there were no disks.
